<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Final exam problems on the dot product
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-exercises-dot-product" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Exercises for <xref ref="section-dot-product" text="title"/></title>

<introduction>
<p>
These problems use the projection of one vector onto another. If you would like to review this topic before
attempting them, see <xref ref="section-dot-product" text="title"/>.
</p>
</introduction>

<exercise xml:id="rs19-4">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two vectors
<m>\mathbf u = 3\,\mathbf j + 4\,\mathbf k</m> and
<m>\mathbf v = \mathbf i + \mathbf j</m>. Write <m>\mathbf u</m> as
the sum of a vector parallel to <m>\mathbf v</m> and a vector
orthogonal to <m>\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
We have <m>\vec u \cdot \vec v = 3</m> and
<m>|\vec v| = \sqrt{1+1} = \sqrt2</m>. The component of
<m>\vec u</m> parallel to <m>\vec v</m> is
<md>
\vec u_{\parallel}
= \operatorname{proj}_{\vec v} \vec u
= \left(\frac{\vec u \cdot \vec v}{|\vec v|^2}\right)\vec v
= \frac32\,(\mathbf i + \mathbf j)
= \frac32\,\mathbf i + \frac32\,\mathbf j,
</md>
and the component orthogonal to <m>\vec v</m> is
<md>
\vec u_{\perp}
= \vec u - \vec u_{\parallel}
= \left(3\,\mathbf j + 4\,\mathbf k\right)
- \left(\frac32\,\mathbf i + \frac32\,\mathbf j\right)
= -\frac32\,\mathbf i + \frac32\,\mathbf j + 4\,\mathbf k.
</md>
Here <m>\vec u_{\parallel}</m> is parallel to <m>\vec v</m> and
<m>\vec u_{\perp}</m> is orthogonal to <m>\vec v</m>. Note that
<m>\vec u_{\parallel} + \vec u_{\perp} = \vec u</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw22-4">
<statement>
<p>
Determine whether the following pairs of vectors are orthogonal,
parallel, or neither.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec u_1 = -3\,\mathbf i + 9\,\mathbf j + 6\,\mathbf k</m> and
<m>\vec u_2 = 4\,\mathbf i - 12\,\mathbf j - 8\,\mathbf k</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec v_1 = \mathbf i + \mathbf j - 2\,\mathbf k</m> and
<m>\vec v_2 = \mathbf i + \mathbf j + \mathbf k</m>
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<m>\vec w_1 = a\,\mathbf i + b\,\mathbf j</m> and
<m>\vec w_2 = c\,\mathbf k</m>, where <m>a</m>, <m>b</m>, and <m>c</m>
are non-zero real numbers.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
<term>Parallel</term>, since <m>\vec u_2 = -\frac43\,\vec u_1</m>:
indeed
<m>-\frac43\langle -3, 9, 6\rangle = \langle 4, -12, -8\rangle</m>.
(Two vectors are parallel exactly when one is a scalar multiple of the
other.)
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<term>Orthogonal</term>, since
<m>\vec v_1 \cdot \vec v_2 = 1 + 1 - 2 = 0</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
<term>Orthogonal</term>, since
<m>\vec w_1 \cdot \vec w_2
= \langle a, b, 0\rangle \cdot \langle 0, 0, c\rangle = 0</m>. They
are not parallel, because <m>\vec w_2</m> has a non-zero
<m>\mathbf k</m> component while <m>\vec w_1</m> does not.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="pp-11">
<statement>
<p>
Find the projection of
<m>-\mathbf i + 5\,\mathbf j - 2\,\mathbf k</m> on
<m>3\,\mathbf i - \mathbf j</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
With <m>\vec u = \langle -1, 5, -2\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec v = \langle 3, -1, 0\rangle</m>,
<md>
\vec u \cdot \vec v = -3 - 5 + 0 = -8,
\qquad
|\vec v|^2 = 9 + 1 + 0 = 10,
</md>
so
<md>
<mrow>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u
\amp = \left(\frac{\vec u \cdot \vec v}{|\vec v|^2}\right)\vec v
= -\frac{8}{10}\langle 3, -1, 0\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle -\frac{12}{5}, \frac45, 0\right\rangle
= -\frac{12}{5}\,\mathbf i + \frac45\,\mathbf j.</mrow>
</md>
The negative scalar tells us the projection points opposite to
<m>\vec v</m>, i.e. the angle between <m>\vec u</m> and
<m>\vec v</m> is obtuse.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="rw17-4">
<statement>
<p>
A 100-meter dash is run on a track in the direction of the vector
<m>2\,\mathbf i + 6\,\mathbf j</m>. The wind velocity is
<m>\mathbf v = 5\,\mathbf i + 2\,\mathbf j</m> km/hr. The rules say
that a legal wind speed measured in the direction of the dash must not
exceed 5 km/hr.
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
Will the race results be disqualified due to an illegal wind? Justify
your answer.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the velocity vector of the wind in the direction of the dash.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Let <m>\vec d = \langle 2, 6\rangle</m> be the direction of the dash
and <m>\vec v = \langle 5, 2\rangle</m> the wind velocity. Then
<md>
\vec v \cdot \vec d = 10 + 12 = 22,
\qquad
|\vec d| = \sqrt{4 + 36} = \sqrt{40} = 2\sqrt{10}.
</md>
<ol marker="A.">
<li>
<p>
The wind speed measured in the direction of the dash is the scalar
component of <m>\vec v</m> along <m>\vec d</m>:
<md>
\operatorname{comp}_{\vec d}\vec v
= \frac{\vec v \cdot \vec d}{|\vec d|}
= \frac{22}{2\sqrt{10}}
= \frac{11}{\sqrt{10}} \approx 3.48 \text{ km/hr}.
</md>
Since <m>3.48 \lt 5</m>, the wind is legal and the race results will
<em>not</em> be disqualified.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
The velocity vector of the wind in the direction of the dash is the
vector projection
<md>
<mrow>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec d}\vec v
\amp = \left(\frac{\vec v \cdot \vec d}{|\vec d|^2}\right)\vec d
= \frac{22}{40}\langle 2, 6\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle \frac{11}{10}, \frac{33}{10}\right\rangle
= 1.1\,\mathbf i + 3.3\,\mathbf j \text{ km/hr}.</mrow>
</md>
As a check, its length is
<m>\sqrt{1.1^2 + 3.3^2} = 1.1\sqrt{10} = \frac{11}{\sqrt{10}}</m>,
matching part A.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2a-1">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\vec v</m> be the vector from <m>(-4,3,-1)</m> to
<m>(-2,-1,3)</m>.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Find a non-zero unit vector that is orthogonal to <m>\vec v</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the angle between <m>\vec v</m> and
<m>\vec w = 3\mathbf i - \mathbf k</m>.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
First,
<md>
<mrow>\vec v \amp = \left\langle -2-(-4),\ -1-3,\ 3-(-1) \right\rangle
= \left\langle 2,\ -4,\ 4 \right\rangle ,</mrow>
<mrow>\|\vec v\| \amp = \sqrt{4+16+16} = \sqrt{36} = 6 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> We need any non-zero <m>\vec u</m> with
<m>\vec u \cdot \vec v = 0</m>. Writing
<m>\vec u = \left\langle a,b,c\right\rangle</m>, the condition is
<md>
2a - 4b + 4c = 0 .
</md>
This is one equation in three unknowns, so there are infinitely many
answers; we just pick a convenient one. Taking <m>c = 0</m> gives
<m>2a = 4b</m>, so <m>a = 2</m>, <m>b = 1</m> works:
<md>
\vec u = \left\langle 2,\ 1,\ 0 \right\rangle ,
\qquad \vec u \cdot \vec v = 4 - 4 + 0 = 0 . 
</md>
Finally we normalize:
<md>
\hat u = \frac{\vec u}{\|\vec u\|}
       = \frac{1}{\sqrt5}\left\langle 2,\ 1,\ 0 \right\rangle
       = \left\langle \frac{2}{\sqrt5},\ \frac{1}{\sqrt5},\ 0
         \right\rangle .
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> With
<m>\vec w = \left\langle 3, 0, -1 \right\rangle</m> we have
<md>
<mrow>\vec v \cdot \vec w \amp = (2)(3) + (-4)(0) + (4)(-1) = 6 - 4 = 2 ,</mrow>
<mrow>\|\vec w\| \amp = \sqrt{9+0+1} = \sqrt{10} .</mrow>
</md>
Therefore
<md>
\cos\theta = \frac{\vec v \cdot \vec w}{\|\vec v\|\,\|\vec w\|}
           = \frac{2}{6\sqrt{10}} = \frac{1}{3\sqrt{10}}
           \approx 0.1054 ,
</md>
so
<md>
\theta = \cos^{-1}\!\left(\frac{1}{3\sqrt{10}}\right)
       \approx 1.465 \text{ radians} \approx 83.9^{\circ} .
</md>
The angle is slightly less than a right angle, which matches the fact
that the dot product is positive but small.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2a-3">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>A = (0,0,1)</m> and <m>B = (0,2,3)</m>. Find an equation for the
set of all points <m>P = (x,y,z)</m> such that <m>\vec{AP}</m> is
orthogonal to <m>\vec{BP}</m>. Then sketch the resulting surface in
three dimensions.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The two vectors are
<md>
<mrow>\vec{AP} \amp = \left\langle x-0,\ y-0,\ z-1 \right\rangle
= \left\langle x,\ y,\ z-1 \right\rangle ,</mrow>
<mrow>\vec{BP} \amp = \left\langle x,\ y-2,\ z-3 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Orthogonality means the dot product vanishes:
<md>
<mrow>\vec{AP}\cdot\vec{BP} \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>x^2 + y(y-2) + (z-1)(z-3) \amp = 0</mrow>
<mrow>x^2 + y^2 - 2y + z^2 - 4z + 3 \amp = 0 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
Now complete the square in <m>y</m> and in <m>z</m>:
<md>
<mrow>x^2 + \left(y^2 - 2y + 1\right) + \left(z^2 - 4z + 4\right)
  \amp = -3 + 1 + 4</mrow>
<mrow>x^2 + (y-1)^2 + (z-2)^2 \amp = 2 .</mrow>
</md>
</p>

<p>
This is the sphere of radius <m>\sqrt2</m> centred at
<m>(0,1,2)</m>. That centre is exactly the midpoint of <m>A</m> and
<m>B</m>, and the radius is exactly half of
<m>\|\vec{AB}\| = \|\langle 0,2,2\rangle\| = 2\sqrt2</m>. This is the
three-dimensional version of the familiar fact from plane geometry: the
points from which a given segment subtends a right angle form the
circle having that segment as a diameter. See
<xref ref="fig-m2a-sphere"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2a-sphere">
<caption>
The set of points <m>P</m> with <m>\vec{AP} \perp \vec{BP}</m> is the
sphere having <m>\overline{AB}</m> as a diameter, namely
<m>x^2+(y-1)^2+(z-2)^2 = 2</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<description>
<p>
A sphere in three dimensional space with the segment from A at zero,
zero, one to B at zero, two, three drawn as a diameter. A point P is
marked on the sphere and the two segments from P to A and from P to B
meet at a right angle.
</p>
</description>
<latex-image><![CDATA[
\begin{tikzpicture}[x={(-0.50cm,-0.36cm)}, y={(1cm,0cm)}, z={(0cm,1cm)}]
% axes
\draw[->, thick] (-1.6,0,0) -- (2.2,0,0) node[below left] {$x$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,-1.2,0) -- (0,4.0,0) node[right] {$y$};
\draw[->, thick] (0,0,-0.8) -- (0,0,4.6) node[above] {$z$};
% the sphere: centre (0,1,2), radius sqrt(2)
\shade[ball color=mblue, opacity=0.30] (0,1,2) circle (1.4142cm);
% the diameter AB
\coordinate (A) at (0,0,1);
\coordinate (B) at (0,2,3);
\coordinate (P) at (0,2.1,1.15);
\draw[very thick, mred] (A) -- (B);
\draw[very thick, mgreen] (A) -- (P);
\draw[very thick, morange] (B) -- (P);
\fill[mred] (A) circle (1.7pt);
\fill[mred] (B) circle (1.7pt);
\fill[black] (0,1,2) circle (1.4pt);
\fill[mverm] (P) circle (1.7pt);
\node[mred, anchor=east] at (A) {$A(0,0,1)$\;};
\node[mred, anchor=south] at (B) {$B(0,2,3)$};
\node[anchor=south east] at (0,1,2) {$(0,1,2)$\;};
\node[mverm, anchor=west] at (P) {\;$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}
]]></latex-image>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="m2b-1b">
<statement>
<p>
Two vectors <m>\vec A</m> and <m>\vec B</m> are drawn with a common
tail; <m>\vec A</m> points up and to the right, <m>\vec B</m> points up
and to the left, and the angle between them is slightly larger than a
right angle. Sketch and clearly label a diagram showing <m>\vec A</m>,
<m>\vec B</m>, and <m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
To make the picture concrete let us take
<m>\vec A = \left\langle 4, 2 \right\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec B = \left\langle -3, 5 \right\rangle</m>, which have roughly
the directions described. Everything below is a statement about
the <em>geometry</em>, so the same picture is correct for any pair of
vectors in that configuration.
</p>

<p>
The projection of <m>\vec B</m> onto <m>\vec A</m> is
the shadow that <m>\vec B</m> casts on the line through
<m>\vec A</m>, obtained by dropping a perpendicular from the tip of
<m>\vec B</m> to that line. Algebraically,
<md>
<mrow>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B
\amp = \left(\frac{\vec A\cdot\vec B}{\|\vec A\|^2}\right)\vec A
= \left(\frac{-12+10}{20}\right)\left\langle 4,2\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -\frac{1}{10}\left\langle 4,2\right\rangle
= \left\langle -0.4,\ -0.2 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
Because the angle between <m>\vec A</m> and <m>\vec B</m> is
<em>obtuse</em>, the dot product is negative and the projection points
in the direction <em>opposite</em> to <m>\vec A</m>. This is the
feature of the picture the problem is testing. See
<xref ref="fig-m2b-1b"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-m2b-1b">
<caption>
Since the angle between <m>\vec A</m> and <m>\vec B</m> is obtuse,
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B</m> points opposite to
<m>\vec A</m>.
</caption>
<image width="55%">
<description>
<p>
A blue arrow A points up and to the right and a green arrow B points
up and to the left from a common tail. A short dark red arrow along
the line of A points backwards, away from A, with a dashed
perpendicular from the tip of B, representing the projection of B
onto A.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-m2b-1b">
<diagram dimensions="(300,240)" margins="16">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.5,-2.0,5.5,6.0)">
<line at="pb-axis" p1="(-3.5,-1.75)" p2="(5.5,2.75)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5" dash="5 4"/>
<vector at="pb-A" v="(4,2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pb-B" v="(-3,5)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#009E73" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="pb-P" v="(-0.4,-0.2)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<line at="pb-drop" p1="(-3,5)" p2="(-0.4,-0.2)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1.5" dash="4 3"/>
<label at="pb-Al" anchor="(4.1,1.9)" alignment="e" color="#0072B2">
<m>\vec A</m>
</label>
<label at="pb-Bl" anchor="(-3.1,5.1)" alignment="nw" color="#009E73">
<m>\vec B</m>
</label>
<label at="pb-Pl" anchor="(-0.55,-1.35)" alignment="w" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\vec A}\vec B</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Vectors A and B drawn from a common tail, with the projection of B onto A drawn along the line through A, pointing opposite to A since the angle between A and B is obtuse.">
  <annotation ref="pb-A" text="The vector A."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-B" text="The vector B."/>
  <annotation ref="pb-P" text="The projection of B onto A, which points opposite to A because the angle between A and B is obtuse."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="dp-norms-geometry">
<statement>
<p>
Suppose <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> are non-zero vectors
satisfying
<md>
|\mathbf u + \mathbf v| = 5, \qquad |\mathbf u - \mathbf v| = 13,
</md>
and
<md>
|\mathbf u|^2 + |\mathbf v|^2 = 97, \qquad |\mathbf u| = 7 .
</md>
Without finding the coordinates of either vector:
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Compute <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find <m>|\mathbf v|</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the angle <m>\theta</m> between <m>\mathbf u</m> and
<m>\mathbf v</m>.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Determine whether the angle is acute, right, or obtuse.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The whole problem rests on one idea: the square of a length is a dot
product, <m>|\mathbf w|^2 = \mathbf w\cdot\mathbf w</m>. Expanding with
the distributive property,
<md>
<mrow>|\mathbf u + \mathbf v|^2 \amp
= (\mathbf u + \mathbf v)\cdot(\mathbf u + \mathbf v)
= |\mathbf u|^2 + 2\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v + |\mathbf v|^2 ,</mrow>
<mrow>|\mathbf u - \mathbf v|^2 \amp
= (\mathbf u - \mathbf v)\cdot(\mathbf u - \mathbf v)
= |\mathbf u|^2 - 2\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v + |\mathbf v|^2 .</mrow>
</md>
These two identities are the algebraic form of the parallelogram in
<xref ref="fig-dp-parallelogram"/>: the diagonals of the parallelogram
built on <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m> are
<m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m> and <m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> Subtracting the second identity from the first makes
the <m>|\mathbf u|^2</m> and <m>|\mathbf v|^2</m> terms cancel, leaving
only the dot product:
<md>
|\mathbf u + \mathbf v|^2 - |\mathbf u - \mathbf v|^2
= 4\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v .
</md>
Substituting the two given lengths,
<md>
<mrow>5^2 - 13^2 \amp = 4\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v ,</mrow>
<mrow>25 - 169 \amp = 4\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v ,</mrow>
<mrow>-144 \amp = 4\,\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v ,</mrow>
</md>
and therefore <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v = -36</m>.
</p>

<p>
It is worth pausing to check that the data are consistent.
<em>Adding</em> the two identities instead of subtracting gives the
parallelogram law,
<md>
|\mathbf u + \mathbf v|^2 + |\mathbf u - \mathbf v|^2
= 2\left(|\mathbf u|^2 + |\mathbf v|^2\right) ,
</md>
and indeed <m>5^2 + 13^2 = 25 + 169 = 194 = 2(97)</m>, exactly as it
should be.
</p>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> We are told <m>|\mathbf u| = 7</m> and
<m>|\mathbf u|^2 + |\mathbf v|^2 = 97</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>7^2 + |\mathbf v|^2 \amp = 97 ,</mrow>
<mrow>49 + |\mathbf v|^2 \amp = 97 ,</mrow>
<mrow>|\mathbf v|^2 \amp = 48 .</mrow>
</md>
A length is never negative, so
<m>|\mathbf v| = \sqrt{48} = 4\sqrt3 \approx 6.93</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> For non-zero vectors,
<m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v = |\mathbf u|\,|\mathbf v|\cos\theta</m>, so
<md>
<mrow>\cos\theta \amp
= \frac{\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v}{|\mathbf u|\,|\mathbf v|}
= \frac{-36}{(7)\left(4\sqrt3\right)}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = -\frac{9}{7\sqrt3} = -\frac{3\sqrt3}{7}
\approx -0.7423 ,</mrow>
</md>
and therefore
<md>
\theta = \cos^{-1}\!\left(-\frac{3\sqrt3}{7}\right)
\approx 2.407 \text{ radians} \approx 137.9^{\circ} .
</md>
</p>

<p>
<term>(d)</term> Since <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v = -36 \lt 0</m> and
the lengths are positive, <m>\cos\theta \lt 0</m>. Hence
<m>\tfrac{\pi}{2} \lt \theta \lt \pi</m> and the angle is
<term>obtuse</term>. Notice that we could have answered this part
immediately after part (a), without knowing either length: the
<em>sign</em> of the dot product already decides the type of angle.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-dp-parallelogram">
<caption>
The parallelogram built on <m>\mathbf u</m> and <m>\mathbf v</m>. Its
diagonals are <m>\mathbf u + \mathbf v</m>, of length <m>5</m>, and
<m>\mathbf u - \mathbf v</m>, of length <m>13</m>. One short diagonal
together with one long one forces the angle <m>\theta</m> between the
sides to be obtuse.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<description>
<p>
A parallelogram with two vectors u and v drawn from a common corner,
showing the short diagonal u plus v and the long diagonal u minus v,
with an obtuse angle marked between u and v.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-dp-parallelogram">
<diagram dimensions="(640,270)" margins="18">
<coordinates bbox="(-6.8,-1.8,13.8,6.8)">
<line at="dpp-side1" p1="(7,0)" p2="(1.857,4.643)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="5 4"/>
<line at="dpp-side2" p1="(-5.143,4.643)" p2="(1.857,4.643)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="5 4"/>
<vector at="dpp-u" v="(7,0)" tail="(0,0)" stroke="#0072B2" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpp-v" v="(-5.143,4.643)" tail="(0,0)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpp-sum" v="(1.857,4.643)" tail="(0,0)"
        stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpp-diff" v="(12.143,-4.643)" tail="(-5.143,4.643)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<angle-marker at="dpp-theta" points="((7,0),(0,0),(-5.143,4.643))"
              radius="26" clear-background="no">
<m>\theta</m>
</angle-marker>
<label at="dpp-ul" anchor="(3.5,-0.25)" alignment="s" color="#0072B2">
<m>\mathbf u,\ |\mathbf u| = 7</m>
</label>
<label at="dpp-vl" anchor="(-3.1,2.55)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>\mathbf v,\ |\mathbf v| = 4\sqrt3</m>
</label>
<label at="dpp-suml" anchor="(2.0,4.82)" alignment="ne" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\mathbf u+\mathbf v,\ \text{length } 5</m>
</label>
<label at="dpp-diffl" anchor="(6.2,1.55)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>\mathbf u-\mathbf v,\ \text{length } 13</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A parallelogram drawn with two solid vectors from a common corner and two dashed sides completing it. The blue vector u points to the right, the green vector v points up and to the left at an obtuse angle to u, a short dark red diagonal runs from the common corner to the opposite corner, and a long orange diagonal runs from the tip of v to the tip of u.">
  <annotation ref="dpp-u" text="The vector u, of length seven, drawn to the right from the common corner."/>
  <annotation ref="dpp-v" text="The vector v, of length four root three, drawn up and to the left, making an obtuse angle with u."/>
  <annotation ref="dpp-sum" text="The short diagonal, u plus v, of length five, running from the common corner to the far corner of the parallelogram."/>
  <annotation ref="dpp-diff" text="The long diagonal, u minus v, of length thirteen, running from the tip of v to the tip of u."/>
  <annotation ref="dpp-theta" text="The angle theta between u and v, about one hundred thirty eight degrees."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<term>A remark on the data.</term> The two diagonal lengths by
themselves already determine <m>\mathbf u\cdot\mathbf v</m>, and the
condition <m>|\mathbf u|^2 + |\mathbf v|^2 = 97</m> then follows
automatically from the parallelogram law. So the individual lengths are
<em>not</em> pinned down by that data alone: the pair
<m>|\mathbf u| = 4\sqrt3</m>, <m>|\mathbf v| = 7</m> fits equally well
and gives the same angle, while other splits of <m>97</m> give
different angles. This is why the extra condition
<m>|\mathbf u| = 7</m> is needed for parts (b) and (c).
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="dp-constrained-work">
<statement>
<p>
A constant force is
<md>
\mathbf F = \left\langle 2, -1, 3 \right\rangle .
</md>
A particle is constrained to move in directions parallel to the plane
<md>
2x - y + 2z = 0 .
</md>
For a displacement of one unit, the work done by the force is
<m>W = \mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d</m>, where <m>\mathbf d</m> is an
allowed unit direction lying in the plane.
<ol marker="(a)">
<li>
<p>
Find the unit direction in the plane that maximizes the work.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find the maximum possible work per unit distance.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Find one unit direction in the plane for which the work is zero.
</p>
</li>
</ol>
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Reading the coefficients of the plane, a normal vector is
<md>
\mathbf n = \left\langle 2, -1, 2 \right\rangle ,
\qquad |\mathbf n| = \sqrt{4+1+4} = 3 .
</md>
A vector is parallel to the plane exactly when it is orthogonal to
<m>\mathbf n</m>.
</p>

<p>
<term>(a)</term> Decompose the force into a normal piece and a piece
lying in the plane,
<md>
\mathbf F = \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
          + \mathbf F_{\parallel} .
</md>
The first piece comes from the projection formula. Since
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf n \amp
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle\cdot\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= 2(2) + (-1)(-1) + 3(2)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 4 + 1 + 6 = 11 ,</mrow>
</md>
and <m>|\mathbf n|^2 = 9</m>, we get
<md>
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
= \frac{11}{9}\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= \left\langle \frac{22}{9},\ -\frac{11}{9},\ \frac{22}{9}\right\rangle .
</md>
Subtracting,
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F_{\parallel} \amp
= \mathbf F - \operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle
- \left\langle \frac{22}{9},\ -\frac{11}{9},\ \frac{22}{9}\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle -\frac49,\ \frac29,\ \frac59\right\rangle
= \frac19\left\langle -4,\ 2,\ 5 \right\rangle .</mrow>
</md>
As a check, this really is parallel to the plane:
<m>\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\cdot\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle
= -8 - 2 + 10 = 0</m>.
</p>

<p>
Now let <m>\mathbf d</m> be any allowed unit direction. Because
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m> is orthogonal to every
vector lying in the plane,
<md>
W = \mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d
  = \left(\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F
    + \mathbf F_{\parallel}\right)\cdot\mathbf d
  = \mathbf F_{\parallel}\cdot\mathbf d .
</md>
The normal part of the force is simply wasted: it presses the particle
against the constraint and does no work. Writing
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}\cdot\mathbf d
= |\mathbf F_{\parallel}|\,|\mathbf d|\cos\varphi
= |\mathbf F_{\parallel}|\cos\varphi</m>, the largest value occurs when
<m>\cos\varphi = 1</m>, that is when <m>\mathbf d</m> points in the same
direction as <m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>. Since
<md>
\left|\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\right| = \sqrt{16+4+25}
= \sqrt{45} = 3\sqrt5 ,
</md>
the maximizing unit vector is
<md>
\mathbf d_{\max}
= \frac{\left\langle -4,\ 2,\ 5\right\rangle}{3\sqrt5} .
</md>
See <xref ref="fig-dp-constrained-work"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-dp-constrained-work">
<caption>
The force <m>\mathbf F</m> splits into a piece
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m> along the normal, which
does no work, and a much shorter piece <m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>
lying in the plane, which is the only part that can do work. The
picture is drawn to scale:
<m>\left|\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F\right| = \tfrac{11}{3}</m>
while <m>\left|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\right| = \tfrac{\sqrt5}{3}</m>.
</caption>
<image width="85%">
<description>
<p>
A tilted plane through the origin with a normal arrow. A force vector
rises steeply from the origin, almost along the normal; it is resolved
into a long component along the normal and a very short component lying
in the plane.
</p>
</description>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-dp-constrained-work">
<diagram dimensions="(600,310)" margins="16">
<coordinates bbox="(-4.7,-1.6,6.0,4.0)">
<polygon at="dpw-plane"
         points="((-0.70,0.30),(5.30,-1.20),(1.90,1.50),(-4.10,3.00))"
         stroke="#0072B2" thickness="2"
         fill="#0072B2" fill-opacity="0.12"/>
<line at="dpw-dash1" p1="(0.701,3.143)" p2="(1.135,3.034)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<line at="dpw-dash2" p1="(0.701,3.143)" p2="(0.166,1.009)"
      stroke="#7F7F7F" thickness="1" dash="4 3"/>
<vector at="dpw-n" v="(0.316,1.261)" tail="(3.3,0.25)"
        stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
<vector at="dpw-proj" v="(0.535,2.134)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#8B1A4A" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpw-force" v="(0.101,2.243)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<vector at="dpw-par" v="(-0.434,0.109)" tail="(0.6,0.9)"
        stroke="#009E73" thickness="4"/>
<point at="dpw-origin" p="(0.6,0.9)" fill="black"/>
<label at="dpw-originl" anchor="(0.66,0.78)" alignment="se">
<m>O</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-nl" anchor="(3.68,1.56)" alignment="e">
<m>\mathbf n=\left\langle 2,-1,2\right\rangle</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-forcel" anchor="(0.62,3.24)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>\mathbf F</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-projl" anchor="(1.22,3.06)" alignment="ne" color="#8B1A4A">
<m>\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf n}\mathbf F</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-parl" anchor="(0.08,0.97)" alignment="w" color="#009E73">
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>
</label>
<label at="dpw-planel" anchor="(5.1,-1.32)" alignment="s" color="#0072B2">
<m>2x-y+2z=0</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A blue parallelogram represents the plane two x minus y plus two z equals zero, drawn through the origin O. A black arrow off to the right shows the normal direction n. From O, a long dark red arrow rises steeply as the projection of F onto n, a slightly tilted orange arrow of almost the same length is the force F itself, and a very short green arrow lying in the plane is the component of F parallel to the plane. Dashed grey segments complete the rectangle of the decomposition.">
  <annotation ref="dpw-plane" text="The constraint plane, two x minus y plus two z equals zero, passing through the origin."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-n" text="A normal vector n to the plane, with components two, negative one, two."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-force" text="The force F, rising steeply and therefore nearly perpendicular to the plane."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-proj" text="The projection of F onto the normal, the long component that does no work."/>
  <annotation ref="dpw-par" text="The component of F parallel to the plane, very short compared with the normal component; this is the only part of the force that can do work."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<term>(b)</term> The maximum work per unit displacement is the length of
the in-plane component,
<md>
<mrow>W_{\max} \amp = \left|\mathbf F_{\parallel}\right|
= \left|\frac19\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle\right|</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac19\left(3\sqrt5\right) = \frac{\sqrt5}{3}
\approx 0.745 .</mrow>
</md>
We can confirm this directly from the original force:
<md>
<mrow>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d_{\max} \amp
= \left\langle 2,-1,3\right\rangle\cdot
  \frac{\left\langle -4,2,5\right\rangle}{3\sqrt5}
= \frac{-8-2+15}{3\sqrt5}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \frac{5}{3\sqrt5} = \frac{\sqrt5}{3} .</mrow>
</md>
Compare this with <m>|\mathbf F| = \sqrt{14} \approx 3.74</m>: the
constraint costs us about <m>80\%</m> of the force.
</p>

<p>
<term>(c)</term> We want a unit vector
<m>\mathbf d = \left\langle x,y,z\right\rangle</m> satisfying two
conditions at once: it must lie in the plane, and it must do no work.
That is,
<md>
\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf n = 0 \qquad\text{and}\qquad
\mathbf d\cdot\mathbf F = 0 ,
</md>
or explicitly
<md>
<mrow>2x - y + 2z \amp = 0 ,</mrow>
<mrow>2x - y + 3z \amp = 0 .</mrow>
</md>
Subtracting the first equation from the second gives <m>z = 0</m>, and
then <m>2x - y = 0</m>, so <m>y = 2x</m>. Taking <m>x = 1</m> gives the
non-zero vector <m>\left\langle 1,2,0\right\rangle</m>, whose length is
<m>\sqrt{1+4} = \sqrt5</m>. Hence one suitable unit vector is
<md>
\mathbf d_0 = \frac{1}{\sqrt5}\left\langle 1,\ 2,\ 0\right\rangle .
</md>
Indeed <m>\mathbf F\cdot\mathbf d_0
= \tfrac{1}{\sqrt5}(2 - 2 + 0) = 0</m>, and the opposite direction
<m>-\mathbf d_0</m> works equally well. Geometrically, <m>\mathbf d_0</m>
is the direction in the plane perpendicular to
<m>\mathbf F_{\parallel}</m>: moving along it, the particle travels
neither with nor against the usable part of the force.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
